// A single-axis belt-driven linear stage.
//
// The first machine, and chosen because it is not a demo. A bed slinger is
// three of these and a frame; a moving gantry is three of these and a frame. So
// the first thing fabrica assembles is the unit everything else is made of,
// which forces sub-assembly recursion on day one rather than leaving it to be
// retrofitted.
//
// It was written by hand before any code existed, because a file is the
// cheapest possible test of whether joints, anchors and limits are the right
// primitives. Three things it found, all recorded below where they bite:
//
//   1. Anchors come in two kinds. A motor face is a frame; an extrusion slot is
//      a line you may clamp anywhere along. Those are not the same thing and
//      one syntax for both would have been wrong.
//   2. A fastening is not a separate concept from the joint it realises. Every
//      fixed joint already knows what holds it, so the screws live there - and
//      the BOM and the build step fall out of the same declaration.
//   3. The belt closes a loop, which the tree rule forbids. It does not break
//      the rule; it clarifies it. See the route at the bottom.

@proven 1;
@machine "{travel} mm belt-driven linear stage on {rail_profile}";

// Four decisions. The budget matters more than it looks: a machine that needs
// forty questions is not a machine design, it is CAD with a browser on top.
// Travel is the only thing chosen freely - everything else is settled by what
// the stage has to do or what it has to mate with, which is what makes them
// form rather than size.

@parameter travel: length {
    label   = "Travel";
    aspect  = size;
    min     = 100;
    max     = 600;
    default = 300;
    summary = "How far the carriage moves, end to end";
    convention = "The usable stroke, not the length of the beam - the beam is longer by what the motor and idler ends take up.";
}

@parameter rail_profile: standard {
    label   = "Rail";
    aspect  = form;
    family  = extrusion;
    default = "2020";
    summary = "The extrusion the carriage rides on";
    convention = "2020 is the usual choice and stiff enough for most of a metre. 2040 buys stiffness in the direction of the cutting load, not along the travel.";
}

@parameter motor_frame: standard {
    label   = "Motor";
    aspect  = form;
    family  = nema;
    default = "17";
    summary = "The stepper driving the belt";
    convention = "NEMA 17 suits a carriage; NEMA 23 is for a gantry carrying its own weight and a spindle.";
}

@parameter belt_profile: standard {
    label   = "Belt";
    aspect  = form;
    family  = synchronous_belt;
    default = "GT2 2 mm";
    summary = "The belt profile, which settles the pulleys with it";
    convention = "Pick the belt and the pulleys follow. The reverse costs you a pulley you cannot buy a belt for.";
}

// --- what is bought ---------------------------------------------------------
// Cut to length from stock, and the arithmetic is directed: the beam follows
// the travel, never the other way round. 120 is what the two ends take up - the
// motor mount at one end and the idler block at the other - and it is written
// here rather than inside either part because it is a property of putting the
// two together, which is exactly what this file is for.

stock beam   = <extrusion>(profile=rail_profile, length=travel + 120);
stock motor  = <nema>(frame=motor_frame, shaft=5, body=40);
stock t_nut  = <t-nut>(profile=rail_profile, thread="M5");

// --- what is made -----------------------------------------------------------
// Three of these exist in mechanica today. Four do not, and that is the point
// rather than a problem: a machine that cannot be assembled without them is
// evidence of demand, which is what the part portfolio wants and has never
// had. Marked so the list can be read off.

part drive_pulley  = <pulleys/timing-pulley>(profile=belt_profile, teeth=20, bore=5);
part idler_pulley  = <pulleys/timing-pulley>(profile=belt_profile, teeth=20, bore=8, flange=1);
part idler_bush    = <bushings/flanged-bushing>(bore=5, outer_diameter=8, length=7);

part mount         = <brackets/motor-mount>(profile=rail_profile, frame=motor_frame);   // TODO(mechanica)
part idler_block   = <brackets/idler-block>(profile=rail_profile, spindle=5);           // TODO(mechanica)
part carriage      = <carriages/belt-carriage>(profile=rail_profile);                   // TODO(mechanica)
part clamp         = <clamps/belt-clamp>(profile=belt_profile);                         // TODO(mechanica)

// --- how it goes together ---------------------------------------------------
// The beam is ground. Everything else hangs off it, and the direction of every
// arrow is parent to child, which is what makes this a tree rather than a pile
// of relationships - and what makes a build order a traversal of it.

ground beam;

// An extrusion slot is a TRACK: a frame that slides along an axis, so it takes
// a position. A bracket's mounting face is a FACE: a frame, full stop. Pinning
// a track with @ is how a fixed joint uses one.
//
// The fastening lives in the joint because the joint is what it realises. Four
// screws here is a pattern, a BOM line, and a sentence in the build
// instructions, all from one declaration.

joint fixed beam.slot_left@0 -> mount.rail_face {
    fasten = <hardware/cap-screw>(standard="M5", length=10) x4 into t_nut;
}

joint fixed beam.slot_left@(travel + 100) -> idler_block.rail_face {
    fasten = <hardware/cap-screw>(standard="M5", length=10) x4 into t_nut;
}

joint fixed mount.motor_face -> motor.flange {
    fasten = <hardware/cap-screw>(standard="M3", length=8) x4;
}

joint fixed motor.shaft -> drive_pulley.bore {
    fasten = grub;      // a set screw onto the flat, not a through-fixing
}

// The idler turns and nothing drives it, so it is revolute with no limit. The
// bush is what it turns on, which is why it is a joint and not a note.
joint fixed    idler_block.spindle -> idler_bush.bore;
joint revolute idler_bush.outer    -> idler_pulley.bore {
    axis = [0, 0, 1];
}

// The one joint that moves under the user's control. The track is NOT pinned
// with @ here, and that is the whole difference: a prismatic joint consumes the
// freedom the track already has, and the limit says how much of it is usable.
joint prismatic beam.slot_top -> carriage.rail_face {
    axis  = [1, 0, 0];
    limit = [0, travel];
    home  = 0;
}

joint fixed carriage.belt_face -> clamp.carriage_face {
    fasten = <hardware/cap-screw>(standard="M3", length=12) x2 into <hardware/hex-nut>(standard="M3");
}

// --- the belt ---------------------------------------------------------------
// This is where the tree nearly breaks. A belt round two pulleys and a carriage
// closes a loop, and a closed loop is what the tree rule forbids, because
// solving one needs a constraint solver.
//
// It does not break the rule, and working out why was worth the file on its
// own. A belt is not a joint. It is stock, routed through anchors, and its
// LENGTH is derived from the geometry it is routed around - which is directed
// arithmetic, the same kind as the beam following the travel. Nothing is
// solved for; a number is computed from poses the tree already resolved.
//
// So: anything flexible - belt, cable, chain, tube - is routed stock with a
// derived length. If something flexible ever cannot be expressed this way, that
// is the signal a solver is genuinely required rather than merely convenient,
// and it should be taken as one.

route belt = <synchronous-belt>(profile=belt_profile) {
    over    = [drive_pulley.pitch_circle, idler_pulley.pitch_circle];
    anchors = [clamp.belt_a, clamp.belt_b];
}

// What the motor does to the carriage, recorded rather than solved. It is
// arithmetic over the drive pulley's pitch diameter, and it is the number a
// user actually wants off this machine - steps per millimetre - as well as the
// one a kinematic sweep needs to animate it.
//
// Least settled thing in the file. It may want to be a property of the route
// rather than a declaration of its own.

drive motor -> carriage via belt;
